    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm preping a chemistry demo for my students about why cutting onions makes your eyes sting. Can you explain the specific chemical pathway from the moment the knife hits the onion to the actual irritation in the eyes? I need to explain the enzymatic part. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a knife cuts an onion, it ruptures cells containing the enzyme alliinase and the sulfur compound S-alk(en)cysteine sulfoxides, which previously existed in different compartments. The enzyme quickly converts these precursors into syn-propanethial-S-oxide, a volatile gas that drifts into the air. Upon reaching your eyes, this gas reacts with the moisture in your tears to form sulfuric acid, which irritates your nerve endings and causes stinging. To prevent this, you can chill the onion or wear safety goggles to slow the reaction or block the gas entirely.",
      "source": "target",
